Yes. SEO adds the retrieval and ranking layer: it helps determine which version people encounter when they go looking—and which version keeps resurfacing until it feels familiar.

Call the abstraction Memory SEO: designing shared knowledge so it can be found, connected, checked, and remembered. Used responsibly, it helps good ideas travel without disguising where they came from.

SEO tactic	Memory translation
Keyword research	Learn the different words people use for the same idea, including slang, questions, and misconceptions.
Topic clusters	Build a connected map of related concepts, so a person can move from one useful idea to the next.
Internal links	Make the relationships explicit: cause, contrast, example, consequence, analogy.
Backlinks	Show who independently supports or discusses a claim—and whether those sources are actually independent.
Structured data	Attach author, date, evidence, definitions, and revision history so claims don’t float free of context.
Search snippets	Create a short, portable summary. Make it memorable without making it misleading.
Freshness	Update the account when evidence changes; preserve the older version so “updated” doesn’t erase “what changed.”
Canonical pages	Point to the best-supported source while labeling legitimate variations and disagreements.
Technical SEO	Reduce friction: clear titles, plain language, searchable archives, accessible formats.
Engagement	Give people a useful reason to revisit and share the idea—understanding, not outrage or compulsion.

The propagation loop looks like this:

A phrase gets published → search makes it visible → links give it context → repetition makes it familiar → familiarity helps it come to mind → people retell it → their retellings become new sources.

That’s where the wiki and memory layers meet. Search ranking affects what’s easy to retrieve; retrieval shapes what people rehearse; rehearsal can shape what they later remember. It doesn’t remotely guarantee that a claim becomes true, but it can make one version feel like the default.

There’s a dark mirror, too: keyword stuffing becomes repetition without evidence; link farms become manufactured consensus; clickbait becomes a memorable phrase detached from its caveats; cloaking becomes telling different audiences incompatible versions. Those tactics can steer attention while degrading the shared record.

So the Pantheon-ready principle might be:

Make an idea easy to find, hard to misquote, simple to connect, and possible to audit.

That turns SEO from “rank this page” into steward the paths by which an idea enters shared memory.